## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only merging with cause-based triage. This record proposes a replacement for
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject); that historical record remains
unchanged. The desk must adopt this replacement before treating it as its accepted policy.

The [incident narrative](/kb/incidents/4471) says one known parser issue and one queued fix.
Its rendered components disagree: the merged reports include a separate booking-form hint
problem without a ticket of its own. The old [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) is stale, and
its own explorer contradicts its subject-only rule.

This review uses the **2026-10-04 export**, not a live ticket-system connection. The desk
continues working. Counts and issue descriptions are a snapshot, not a claim that nothing
has arrived or changed since then. Obtain a fresh export and rerun before operational closure.

## Problems and tickets

- [Parser problem: GitHub issue 1](https://github.com/0zminDev/mega-s01-e03/issues/1).
  The shared parser assumes month first for day-first tenants. Fix the explicit parsing
  contract and test calendar-invite round trips.
- [Form hint problem: GitHub issue 2](https://github.com/0zminDev/mega-s01-e03/issues/2).
  For day-first tenants on booking-v3, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` but reads the tenant's
  day-first order. Make its visible hint agree with its parser. A shared-parser fix alone
  does not repair this form contract.

The following table computes the reports covered by each problem from unique report IDs.
Its final column describes the **original desk ticket**, not the new GitHub issues above.
Do not use the intake tags as the cause: they are guesses based on the reporter's browser.
Resolve the report's tenant locale and booking-form version instead.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The one-day-move footnote is evidence of uncertainty inside the parser bucket, not proof of
another established root cause. Keep those report IDs visible in the parser investigation.
Check original inputs, invite timestamps, timezone offsets and corrections before either
claiming a timezone cause or calling these reports fixed. If a distinct mechanism is confirmed,
open its own ticket and reassign IDs without double counting.

## Received is not merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The July merge spike is desk processing of older reports, not a July arrival spike or evidence
that a fix worked. Compare `receivedAt` with `mergedAt`; neither timestamp proves resolution.
The monthly audit below and the weekly chart both deduplicate report IDs.

<ReportFlow ticket={4471} />

## How to triage the next report

1. Read the report and preserve the original input, expected day and actual day.
2. Resolve the tenant's configured locale and form version; browser locale and intake tags
   are hints, not authority. If the tenant is unknown, investigate instead of merging.
3. Distinguish the shared parser from the booking-v3 hint mismatch using the channel and
   form contract. Check anomalous symptoms rather than assuming every report in a bucket
   has been explained.
4. Merge only when the mechanism matches an existing ticket. Equal subjects do not prove
   equal causes; different subjects do not prove different causes.
5. Count each report ID once, link the appropriate issue, and check a fresh export before
   changing counts or closing either problem.

## Counterexamples to subject-only merging

The explorer below executes the existing cause rules. Same-subject reports can need separate
tickets when channel or tenant/form differs. Different subjects can share a cause. Unknown
tenants must remain unplaced, even when their subjects match.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Evidence boundary

The export supplies a classifier and symptom reports, not production parser source or a
reproducible live product. These tickets distinguish the supported mechanisms; they do not
assert deployed fixes. Keep the original incident narrative and accepted decision as historical
evidence rather than rewriting them to make the contradiction disappear.

